WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.895
Welcome to Module Six: Prompt Engineering.

2
00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:06.333
It's about getting more out of a model without touching its weights.

3
00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:11.200
Picture an IT team building an assistant for their internal runbooks.

4
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.680
There's no training budget, so fine-tuning is off the table.

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.798
Their first attempt is a one-line prompt.

6
00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:22.089
The model rambles, invents a server name, and returns broken JSON.

7
00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.332
None of that needs new weights.

8
00:00:24.662 --> 00:00:28.004
It needs a better prompt, better decoding, and the right documents.

9
00:00:28.804 --> 00:00:31.229
Lesson one starts with in-context learning.

10
00:00:31.559 --> 00:00:36.586
A frozen model picks up a task from examples in its prompt, with no gradient update.

11
00:00:37.386 --> 00:00:41.688
Zero-shot gives just the instruction, while few-shot adds worked examples.

12
00:00:42.018 --> 00:00:45.829
How you choose and order those examples matters more than you'd expect.

13
00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.929
Chat models expect system, user, and assistant roles.

14
00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:53.878
The chat template turns them into the tokens the model was trained on.

15
00:00:54.678 --> 00:00:57.657
Lesson two makes the model reason before it answers.

16
00:00:57.987 --> 00:00:59.787
Chain-of-thought asks for steps,

17
00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:02.908
and self-consistency samples several chains and votes.

18
00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:05.962
Then you'll control how each token is picked.

19
00:01:06.292 --> 00:01:11.213
Temperature reshapes probabilities, while top-k and top-p trim the unlikely tail.

20
00:01:12.013 --> 00:01:15.163
In a short lab, you'll run one prompt under different settings.

21
00:01:15.493 --> 00:01:19.517
Seeds, stop sequences, and token limits make results repeatable.

22
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:23.382
For the tickets, asking nicely for JSON isn't enough.

23
00:01:23.712 --> 00:01:29.422
Constrained decoding in vLLM only allows tokens that keep the output schema-valid.

24
00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:35.335
Lesson three adds your own documents with retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.

25
00:01:35.665 --> 00:01:38.623
You'll chunk runbooks, embed them, and index them with FAISS.

26
00:01:39.423 --> 00:01:42.329
At question time, the closest chunks are retrieved,

27
00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:44.301
reranked, and cited in the prompt.

28
00:01:44.631 --> 00:01:49.167
Mixing keyword and dense search catches exact names and paraphrases alike.

29
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:55.485
NVIDIA's NeMo Retriever serves embedding and reranking models as NIM microservices.

30
00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:59.840
Grounding instructions and citations keep answers tied to sources.

31
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.369
Now the assistant answers from the runbook, cites it, and returns valid JSON.

32
00:02:05.699 --> 00:02:08.593
And when nothing relevant is found, it says it doesn't know.

33
00:02:09.393 --> 00:02:12.921
By the end, you'll build a RAG pipeline and know when to prompt,

34
00:02:12.971 --> 00:02:14.207
retrieve, or fine-tune.

35
00:02:14.537 --> 00:02:18.433
Prompting is thirteen percent of the exam, so let's start with Lesson one.
